perating the application needs to be  able to answer these questions  as well in very fine detail  so we can give you  at a glance answers to all three of  these questions even for one of our  biggest customers so i want to  illustrate this with waze carpool  um waze carpool launched i think in  california last year and then in  washington  earlier this year and it allows drivers  to pair up with riders  and for a nominal compensation to be  exchanged between the two so that  you can carpool effectively to work  spend time with people you enjoy so you  can set preferences on who you drive  with  and get there faster in the hov lane and  reduce commute costs  so this is what waze cost look like in  our reports dashboard  earlier this year so they had this is  kind of a nice view because it also  shows you a general pattern with what  cloud costs usually look like which is  compute costs tend to be the large cost  and that makes sense  um and there's cyclicality and  periodicity in your in your costs  it may correspond to daily cyclicality  so waze has that  with date morning and evening commutes  or like seasonal cyclicality  retail retail periods other things like  that  but in general there's a longer term  secular trend of those costs  that also keeps the cloud administrator  confident that their  environment isn't running out of control  that they don't have runaway costs  um but you know just at a glance you can  see something happened  earlier last year where there was a big  spike i i presume waze investigated that  because that doesn't look like it was  normal  a couple maybe a few months ago there  was a smaller spike may or may not have  been normal and that's worth  investigating so just at a glance  waze can log into the cloud console on  gcp and see what's going on with their  service  and again a service that serves over 100  million users  where they've driven you know 22 and a  half billion kilometers  is all kind of represented by the  infrastructure on this chart  so then they launched waze carpool um  and this was the trend after they  launched ways carpool  and so immediately you've we've answered  um at a high level the three questions  what are what's my spending level  so you have the spending level at any  moment of time what's the trend and you  have this  short-term trend but also the long-term  trend which is more interesting one of  the questions i would want to ask myself  launching a new services  is the cost growth good is it growing  sublinearly with customer demand  is it in control or is something  happening where our costs are  spiraling out of control compared to the  demand we expected um  and this shows kind of a nice story of  what the secular growth was before they  launched this major new feature and what  it looked like  after they launched the feature and you  can also see that nothing big  changed um about their makeup at least  at a glance here  compute costs still seem to be the same  fraction bigquery analysis cost cloud  storage  and so forth so just this visual  understanding of cloud cost is very  powerful because  the visual explanation is just a really  good way to communicate the structure of  costs  so i just want to do one footnote before  we head into how to track  cloud costs because there's really two  ways to go about it  one way is tops down you could use the  resource hierarchy and put resources in  a project everything on google cloud  platform is assigned to a project and  you  can set up an organization folders under  that organization  projects under each folder and then  resources within a project  and users operate within projects they  have permissions to create resources  within projects and so if you've created  a nice project hierarchy and you've  thought well about it  you know you could have a project that's  corresponds to an application and then  all the costs in that project  are application costs or you could have  a project that captures  all of an environment like the dev  environment so all the costs in that  project would be the dev environment  costs  and so forth the other way to track  costs is bottoms up through labels  and labels apply to resources so you can  ask your developers or set up tools that  automatically attach labels to resources  that are created  and then you can get basically made kind  of matrix allocation of costs you might  say okay within  project a i know my costs are x  in total but i also know that you know  the environment  prod in project a cost in some fraction  of x environment dev  cost some other fraction and so on and  that's how you can use labels  um you can also attach other important  metadata like who's the owner of the  resource  um what's the cost center things like  that so these are two things to think  about  as you're about how you want to monitor  and manage costs in google cloud  platform  so we're going to focus a lot of today  on billing reports  but i just want to make you aware of a  couple of other  major features that might be useful to  you in monitoring and managing  cloud costs one is billing export the  other is budgets and alerts  and the last one which we'll do kind of  an in-depth demo on is billing reports  so billing export is really where you  would kind of just dive in  um write detailed queries and kind of  answer customized questions about your  cloud costs  all of the labels go into billing export  you see what service was used  what sku was used details about  the project and and the sku and over  time we'll be adding more and more  information to bigquery export  and what's cool about this is you can  connect to other tools like data studio  there's another talk we gave explaining  how to do that  and so this really allows you to answer  fine-grained questions about  cloud costs the next feature we have  budgets and alerts is really worth  taking a look at  you can set budgets at the billing  account or project level  and then set thresholds for when you  want to be notified  about that budget and the notification  can happen via email  or programmatically via pub subs so  email's great for cloud administrators  who just want to be aware of things and  may not be operating a large scale  operation but pub sub and programmatic  notifications are especially cool if  you're running a really large  application or if you're technically  savvy  and can set up really simple responses  to those notifications like oh  if project a exceeds 90 please start  throttling project a  and that unleashes a very cool solutions  for your organization  and matt and lucas carlson from the  broad gave a very cool talk about that  earlier it'll be on youtube  so now i'll turn it over to phil and  he'll walk you through billing reports  so now we're going to dive into the  billing reports tool in a little bit  more detail  and we'll do that through the lens of  our hypothetical enterprise acme company  uh and they want to answer the same  questions as most other i.t  organizations  what are my costs what are my cost  trends and what are my cost drivers  but before we actually look at the tool  in too much great detail  a quick show of hands who here has used  google cloud billing reports in the  cloud console  looks like maybe 30 okay so  for some of you uh you may have already  seen this tool and used it  for others of you it may be familiar  given some other bi tools that you've  used  and for many of us this may be first  time seeing it so we'll learn something  can we cut back over to the demo please  fantastic um first let's just take a  quick look at the tool  um i'll give a quick walk through and  then we can look at answering those  questions  so for starters we can get to this by  going to  the billing tab and the reports section  right at the top here of the page we  have a summary bar  which includes high level information  about what our costs are  for our app below that we have a  chart that lets us analyze trends and  see how  our usage breaks down and then at the  bottom we have a table  that summarizes and rolls up the  information from that chart  lastly on the right section of the page  we have grouping and filter controls  that let us slice and dice our data  and filter down to the scope of spend  that's relevant for us to look at  okay so to answer those questions first  what are our costs  the summary bar gives us a really nice  concise answer to that  so far in the first 26 days of july  for our demo here we've spent 1400  for the entire month of july looking at  the right half of the summary bar we can  see that we're forecasted to spend about  eighteen  hundred dollars so we can see easily  that the summary bar it gives us a  really nice quick answer  that we can answer that question in  maybe five seconds  next what are our cost trends  in the first 26 days of july it looks  like our costs are down about three and  a half percent over the preceding 26 day  period  but for the entire month of july it  looks like we're forecasted to spend  about eight and a half percent more than  we did in the month of june this is  because we're growing  also because july has one more day than  june so we would expect costs to go up a  little bit anyways  but to look at these trends in more  detail i'm going to look down at the  chart  a few things here to note one um we have  a stacked line chart  so the total height of the stacked line  is  what we're spending um on a daily level  but each one of these segments  represents a meaningful portion of our  spend  um here we're grouping by project so  each one of these colored segments  represents the spend for a single  project  at acme company we organize our  applications into projects so each one  of these colored segments  is how much we're spending on one  application for one day  lastly we have a dotted gray line here  that represents  our cost trend and this is how we get to  this forecasted cost number for the  month  um the forecasted cost is the sum  of our actual spend to date and our  forecasted spend for  the remainder of the month  um but to look at i want to explain a  little bit how we get to this cost  trend number it's a feature we've rolled  out recently  and it's pretty powerful so i'm going to  select the custom date range here  just so that we can get a little more  context  i'll look at the period from may 1st  to august 25th okay  so for the purposes of the demo we  created this account sometime in mid-may  and the account grew along with our  hypothetical demand  and the cost trend picks up on that  long-term secular stable trend  and it projects it out up to about a  month into the future  so the way this works is we take the  entire  history for the billing account all the  spend that you've had to date and we  look for  the most recent stable long-term trend  so  maybe if you launched the new  application like waze carpool it would  start at that point or if you went  through a major cost optimization effort  it might start after that  it also does a good job of ignoring  spikes like we see here in the middle of  june  so it really just picks up on whatever  your organic growth trend is  and will forecast that forward into the  future  great so now we kind of have a quick  answer of our first two questions  what are my costs and what are my cost  trends  but we've answered this only for the  entire billing account  what if we want to know a little bit  more about a single application in this  case  let's say phil's amazing application not  to toot my own horn too much  um we can answer the same questions just  by filtering down to that one project  we can see that so far we've spent 450  on the application this month  we're expected to spend about 600 in the  month of july  and that represents about a 36 percent  growth  over the month of june  okay  so lastly the one the one question we  haven't really dove into too much yet is  what are my cost  drivers um so to do that i want to  go back to those spikes that we saw in  june i've selected the previous month  here so we can look at those in a bit  more detail  so here we see a spike around june 8th  and we see another spike around june  11th  and i want to know at a high level what  changed to cause those spikes  the chart's a really powerful tool for  doing this and we can quickly see just  by hovering over this section that  this orange slice which was our good  enough project that really changed a lot  it grew at that time  and it also grew for the june 11th spike  on that june 11 spike we also see that  we had some growth in this  blue segment which was phil's amazing  application but i can get a little more  detail i want to understand  what changed in those applications to  cause those spikes  and let's look at good enough project  first so i'll filter down to just that  project  and then i want to ask the question  which products or services  changed to cause the cost to increase so  i'll group  by product instead of by project  and now i can see that bigquery all of a  sudden went from nothing to 28  overnight and that may have been  unexpected it may have been something we  planned for  we can get even a little more detail and  see what happened in bigquery  by filtering down to just that product  and grouping by skew to see if there are  any trends in the skus that we were  using  so not surprisingly it's analysis not  storage that  fluctuated really highly up and then  came back down um  and at this point this is about as much  detail as we can get from this report  but we might want to go talk to say our  data science team  and learn what kinds of queers they were  running and if you know they were  pulling a data set to build a new  predictive model that we get a lot of  value out of that may be totally fine  if however we want to trade off some of  the flexibility  for a little more predictable and  controllable costs we might want to look  into something like  flat rate bigquery pricing  okay  so now i want to go through a similar  process and try to understand what  happened in phil's amazing application  in the middle of june so again  i'm going to filter down to just phil's  amazing application  and we can see just by hovering over the  chart we were spending about eight  dollars a day  and then it jumped up to about 35 a day  sometime in mid-june  and that's something i'd want to know  say what products what services changed  so again i'll filter down to just  compute engine  sorry jumped ahead  i would group by product and i would see  what products changed  in this case we can see in phil's  amazing application  we run two products or services both app  engine and compute engine  and compute engine is where the change  occurred and again we can get a little  more detail by looking at what our  changes were in our skus  so here i'll just filter down to compute  engine and group by skew  and there's a couple changes that we can  see here during that spike  normally just by hovering over i can see  we run n1 standard ones which is our  smallest standard machine type of the n1  family  but then right here in the middle of the  month  we see a couple other flavors of sku and  one standard eights and then one  standard 16s which are bigger and more  expensive than what we normally run  and again this is about as much detail  as you can get from a high level report  but you might want to dive into more  detailed data  in like your stack driver logs or in our  bigquery billing export  or we might go talk to the project owner  or the engineer and say hey  is there a reason why we're running  these bigger machines  and why we didn't spend them down for a  few days afterwards  additionally when we see something like  that if it's unexpected  we may want to set up a passive  monitoring solution like  budgets and alerts so that we're  notified if our spend trends quicker  than we expect at this point we can cut  back over to the slides  so now we've gone through a demo of  billing reports we've seen how we can  use this tool to really quickly get a  high level answer to our three key cost  management questions  and we've also seen how we can get to a  certain level of depth  at which point we need to go to either a  more detailed  data source like our billing export or  like  stackdriver logs to get even more rich  detail about say machine utilization  or we can go talk to the relevant teams  to figure out what happened when we see  spikes like we did in the middle of june  so that concludes our demo um before we  wrap up  i just want to highlight a couple of  other sessions related to cost  management and billing  on tuesday we had a talk by james byers  on how to  organize and structure your resources  which also relates to the slide that  steven went through earlier about labels  versus  projects and folders yes  we also had a pretty cool talk yesterday  with dale and dale from vendasta where  they did some interesting live demos  about how to use  bigquery export and data studio to slice  and dice your data  and lastly matt and lucas carlson  talked a bit about how we can control  our cloud costs with financial  governance tools  we also have a showcase downstairs on  the bottom floor of moscone west  please stop by and say hi we'll be there  probably after this presentation  [Music]  you
 

